{
  "comment": "Generated by runtime/http/go/webhook (cd runtime/http/go && go test ./webhook -run TestWriteParityVectors -update); do not edit. The Go, TypeScript and Rust HTTP runtimes each run every vector through their JWT body-hash webhook verifier (D81): webhook.JWTBodyHashVerifier in Go, jwtBodyHashVerifier in TypeScript, JwtBodyHashVerifier in Rust. Build the verifier from verifier (seconds and bytes as each runtime takes them) with the clock at the vector's now (unix seconds), a fresh cache, and a key lookup that fails when lookupFails, answers each kid in keys with the public JWK (kty EC, crv P-256, kid, use sig, alg ES256) of a P-256 key the run generates (the trusted key), and answers no key for any other kid. Mount it in front of a POST route that answers 200 with the body it reads, and POST body (UTF-8) with Content-Type application/json and, unless token is null, the header verifier.header. The header's value is token.raw when it is set. Otherwise it is token.signingInput, a dot, and the base64url of a signature: the ES256 signature (r||s, 64 bytes) of token.signed, or of token.signingInput when signed is empty, by token.signer, trusted, or stranger (a second key the run generates, which the lookup never answers), or none (no signature, so the token ends in a dot); then token.tamper, when set, changes it: flip XORs its last byte with 1, truncate drops its last byte. token.header and token.claims are the two JSON objects signingInput encodes, for reading. Compare the status and want.code, the problem code of a refusal (null on 200); on 200 the route read body exactly.",
